  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-019',
    number: 127,
    month: 3,
    question: 'What are chromosomal conditions, explained simply?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 3 (weeks 9 to 13)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Chromosomes are tiny structures inside every cell that carry genetic instructions; most people have 46, in 23 pairs. A chromosomal condition happens when a baby has an extra or missing chromosome, or an extra piece of one, which is what tests like the NT (nuchal translucency) scan and double marker test are screening for.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Understanding this makes all the screening vocabulary easier to follow. Down syndrome, for example, happens when there are three copies of chromosome 21 instead of two, which doctors call trisomy 21. Edwards syndrome and Patau syndrome work the same way, with an extra copy of chromosome 18 or 13. These usually happen by chance during the formation of the egg or sperm, not because of anything a parent did.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Screening tests estimate the chance of these conditions; only a diagnostic test like CVS (chorionic villus sampling) or amniocentesis, which examines the baby\'s actual chromosomes, can confirm one. Most pregnancies, including most flagged as higher risk on screening, do not have a chromosomal condition.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'If any of your results mention a specific chromosome or condition, ask your doctor or a genetic counsellor to explain exactly what it means for your situation.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'These terms can sound frightening in isolation, but understanding what they actually describe usually makes them feel far less overwhelming.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Chromosomes carry genetic instructions; most people have 46.',
        'A chromosomal condition means an extra, missing, or altered chromosome.',
        'Down syndrome is an extra copy of chromosome 21.',
        'These usually happen by chance, not due to anything a parent did.',
        'Only a diagnostic test can confirm a condition; screening only estimates chance.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-029',
      'PREG-SCAN-032',
      'PREG-HEALTH-025',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-SCAN-031',
    number: 128,
    month: 3,
    question: 'What are "soft markers" on an early scan, and do they mean something is wrong?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'Month 3 (weeks 9 to 13)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A soft marker is a small feature the sonographer notices on a scan, such as a nasal bone that looks thin or is hard to see, that is slightly more common in babies with chromosomal conditions. It is never read alone: your doctor weighs it alongside your NT (nuchal translucency) measurement, blood test results and age, and on its own a soft marker is not a diagnosis and often turns out to be a normal variation.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The nasal bone, for example, is only part bone; the rest of the nose is cartilage and soft tissue, so a bone that looks thin or unclear on a scan says nothing about how the nose will actually look or function. Soft markers are simply patterns doctors have noticed occur somewhat more often, statistically, alongside certain chromosomal conditions, not physical problems in themselves.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Most babies with an isolated soft marker and otherwise reassuring results are perfectly healthy. It is not something you can prevent or cause, and there is nothing to do differently in the meantime beyond waiting for your combined result.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor exactly which marker was noted and how it factors into your overall combined result.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A "soft" marker is called that for good reason: it nudges a risk calculation slightly, but it never speaks for your baby on its own.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A soft marker is a small scan feature, not a diagnosis.',
        'The nasal bone is a commonly mentioned example.',
        'It is always weighed alongside your NT measurement, blood tests and age.',
        'Most babies with an isolated soft marker are healthy.',
        'It is not something a parent causes or can prevent.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-027',
      'PREG-HEALTH-019',
      'PREG-SCAN-029',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-020',
    number: 129,
    month: 3,
    question: 'Does my age change my screening options?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'Month 3 (weeks 9 to 13)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Your age is automatically factored into your combined screening result, but it does not change which tests are available to you. The same NT scan, double marker test and optional NIPT (Non-Invasive Prenatal Testing) are offered to every pregnant woman, regardless of age.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Doctors commonly use 35 as a reference point because the background chance of certain chromosomal conditions rises gradually from around this age, though it remains low in absolute terms; commonly cited figures put it at around 1 in 400 at 35, rising further with each additional year. This is simply one more input, alongside your NT scan and blood test results, that feeds into your one combined risk figure.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You are not required to have any additional or different tests because of your age. Some women, at any age, choose to go straight to NIPT or a diagnostic test for more certainty and peace of mind, which is a personal choice, not something tied to a specific birthday.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor how your age factors into your specific result, and what your options are if you would like more certainty than screening alone provides.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Age is one number among several that make up your result; it never tells the whole story on its own.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Age is automatically factored into your combined screening result.',
        'The same tests remain available to everyone, regardless of age.',
        '35 is a commonly used reference point, not a strict cutoff.',
        'Chance rises gradually with age but stays low in absolute terms.',
        'Choosing NIPT or a diagnostic test is personal, not age-dependent.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-029',
      'PREG-SCAN-032',
      'PREG-SCAN-030',
