  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-022',
    number: 132,
    month: 3,
    question: 'What is thalassemia carrier screening, and do I need it?',
    category: 'Tests & Reports',
    applicability: 'Month 3 (weeks 9 to 13), ideally by first trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (light)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Thalassemia carrier screening is a simple blood test that checks whether you carry a gene for thalassemia, a blood disorder that is notably common in India. It matters because if both parents are carriers, there is a real chance of a more serious condition in the baby, so early screening gives couples time to understand their options.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Thalassemia carrier rates are meaningfully high in parts of India, in some communities affecting around one in ten people, which is why many doctors recommend screening for all pregnant women here rather than only those with a known family history. Carrying the trait yourself usually causes no symptoms at all; the concern only arises when both partners carry it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'The test is usually a simple blood test, sometimes starting with basic blood counts and confirmed with a more detailed test called HPLC (high performance liquid chromatography) if needed. If you are found to be a carrier, your partner should also be tested, since it is the combination of both parents\' results that matters, not either alone. Being a carrier yourself is common and not something to be alarmed about.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor whether this screening is included in your antenatal blood tests, and if you are a carrier, what testing your partner should have.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This is quietly one of the most useful tests in Indian antenatal care; it turns an invisible, common trait into information a family can actually plan around.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A blood test checking for the thalassemia carrier gene.',
        'Notably common in parts of India, sometimes around 1 in 10 people.',
        'Being a carrier alone usually causes no symptoms.',
        'Risk to the baby arises only if both parents are carriers.',
        'If you are a carrier, your partner should be tested too.',
      ]),
    ],
