    month: 3,
    question: 'What if I missed the NT scan window? What are my options now?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'Month 3 (weeks 9 to 13), for those past the window',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'If you are past 13 weeks and 6 days, the NT (nuchal translucency) scan itself can no longer be done, but you still have good options: a second trimester blood test called the quadruple marker test, done between 15 and 20 weeks, or NIPT (Non-Invasive Prenatal Testing), which has no such cutoff and can still be done.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The fluid the NT scan measures is naturally reabsorbed by the baby\'s body by around 14 weeks, which is a real biological change, not a rule set by doctors; after this point, there is simply nothing left to measure accurately.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'The quadruple marker test is slightly less accurate than the first trimester combined screening, but it remains a genuinely useful option, and NIPT is more accurate than either. Missing the original window is common and nothing to feel behind on; simply speak to your doctor about which alternative suits your stage and preferences.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor which alternative test fits your current week and situation best.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A missed window is a scheduling gap, not a missed chance; good alternatives exist precisely because pregnancies do not always follow a perfect calendar.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The NT scan cannot be done after about 13 weeks 6 days.',
        'The quadruple marker test (15 to 20 weeks) is one alternative.',
        'NIPT has no strict cutoff and remains an option.',
        'Missing the window is common; it is not something to worry about.',
        'Ask your doctor which option suits your current stage.',
      ]),
