  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-SCAN-034',
    number: 138,
    month: 3,
    question: 'Is first trimester screening different for twin pregnancies?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'Month 3 (weeks 9 to 13), twin pregnancies',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, screening works a little differently with twins. Combined screening (NT scan and double marker test) remains the recommended first-line option, but the blood test is somewhat less predictive with two babies, and the NT scan becomes especially important to look at each baby individually.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'With two babies, hormone levels in your blood are roughly double what a single-baby reference range expects, so labs need twin-specific calculations to avoid false alarms. It also matters whether the twins share one placenta or have separate ones, since a shared placenta makes interpretation more complex. And because the blood test measures an average across both babies, it cannot reliably tell which twin, if either, might be affected, which is exactly why the NT scan, looking at each baby directly, carries extra weight here.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Make sure your scan centre is aware you are carrying twins, and confirm your blood test is being interpreted using twin-specific reference ranges. Any result should be discussed with your doctor in the context of your specific twin pregnancy rather than a general result.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor how your twin pregnancy affects the interpretation of your specific results.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Screening for twins asks slightly more of the process, which is simply a reflection of there being two little stories to follow instead of one.',
